  "claim": "On one MacBook Pro (Apple M5 Pro, 24 GB, macOS 26.5.1) with gitleaks 8.30.1, the 2026-09-24 09:45 Jetsam events were reproduced: a staged generated explorer (one 12,889,851-byte line, 2,335 findings) drives the global pre-commit hook's `gitleaks git --pre-commit --staged --redact` past 6 GiB in about 17 s, and the recipe's all-refs `gitleaks git --redact=100` past 8 GiB in 36 s, because upstream redaction copies the whole line for every finding. Through adoption/tools/gitleaks-guarded-macos the same scans stop at the 6 GiB footprint cap with exit 137 (the hook then blocks the commit), concurrent scans run one at a time under the per-user lock, and dir scans, the CI-scope history scan and small hook scans keep their upstream results and exit codes.",
  "limitations": [
    "One host and one repository state (source 5af3581); a different history, staged content or Gitleaks release can change every peak.",
    "The guard is a user-space watchdog that samples the process tree's physical footprint every 50 ms, not a kernel limit: measured overshoot was 0.04-0.20 GiB above the 6 GiB cap across the development and final passes, and a scan keeps running unwatched if the guard itself is SIGKILLed (it still holds the lock).",
    "Measured with the lock at a scratch path (ECOSYSTEM_GITLEAKS_LOCK); the default per-user lock file, installation on PATH and the global hook change were not installed on this host by this unit and remain the agent-ecosystem owner's step.",
    "No CPU or task cap on macOS: the killed history scan used about 16 cores for 37 s.",
    "Scans that exceed the cap produce no result (exit 137). This is fail-closed, not coverage: the all-refs history of this repository still has no complete redacted Gitleaks pass on macOS.",
    "The incident attribution (a test scratch commit through the global hook, before #181 made the tests hermetic) is inferred from process ages, coalitions and session records; the Jetsam reports carry no argv.",
    "Raw stderr, reports and the Jetsam files stay on the host; published values are sanitized (paths replaced by placeholders, no process or session identifiers)."
  ],
